 "caveats": [
  "Sampling bias: records and raw species counts show where people looked (coasts, rich countries, research stations, shipping lanes) as much as where species live. Records and species per cell correlate strongly (see stats).",
  "ES50 corrects for the number of records, not for where sampling happened or which groups were sampled: a cell sampled only for plankton or only for birds gets the ES50 of that group. Cells with fewer than 50 records have no ES50.",
  "speciesgrids merges OBIS and GBIF records; datasets published to both can be counted twice, and GBIF bird observations (eBird) make up a large share of coastal records.",
  "Taxonomy follows the World Register of Marine Species at build time; names change (synonyms are merged into the accepted name), and misidentified or mislocated records are not removed beyond OBIS quality flags.",
  "Records include museum specimens and observations from 1700s to 2026; a record is not proof the species lives there today. Absence of records is not absence of species.",
  "AquaMaps richness is a model (environmental envelopes + expert ranges), not observations; it is smooth by construction and excludes species with fewer than 10 good occurrence cells."
 ],
